%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
title: b1.1 What residents and staff value
syntax: xwiki/2.1
%%WIKI-SYNC-META%%
**Abstract.** Eight human values ground the social introductions concept, held by the stakeholders in [[a2.1 Residents, family and staff>>doc:1 Foundation.a2 Stakeholders.a2\.1 Residents, family and staff.WebHome]]. Social connectedness leads: residents want genuine company and, over time, friendships with peers. Autonomy and privacy set its boundaries, ensuring each resident chooses whom to meet, when to stay in their room, and what biographical details others may learn. Dignity demands that forgotten names or conversational slips pass without exposure. Truthfulness rules out deception—including well-meant white lies—and reserves painful truths for a trusted human caregiver.

= Human Value - Autonomy [HV-01] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HV-01
|Name|Autonomy
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.
|Situated meaning|Each resident decides whether to engage with the System, whom to meet, when to join communal spaces, and what to do together. They control which biographical details may be shared with peers and can revise those choices at any time. Explicit consent precedes every introduction and every shared personal fact.
|Held by|ST-01, ST-02 (high); ST-05 (medium)
|Intrinsic or instrumental|Intrinsic; also supports Dignity (HV-02).
|Recognition criteria|(((
Served: residents configure and update their own preferences; declining an invitation or turning away ends the interaction immediately without anyone asking for a justification.

Violated: pairings arranged for staff convenience; personal details mentioned without prior consent; repeated persuasion after a refusal; reminders or tallies of declined invitations.
)))
|Source|Ryan, R. M. & Deci, E. L. (2000). Self-determination theory and the facilitation of intrinsic motivation, social development, and well-being. //American Psychologist//, 55(1), 68-78.

= Human Value - Dignity [HV-02] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HV-02
|Name|Dignity
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.
|Situated meaning|Residents are addressed as capable adults. A forgotten name or mid-sentence pause passes without remark, and nobody is quizzed, corrected, or praised for basic participation. Companionship is offered for the resident's own sake, never to fill an institutional schedule.
|Held by|ST-01 (high); ST-02 (medium)
|Intrinsic or instrumental|Intrinsic.
|Recognition criteria|(((
Served: names are woven naturally into greetings for both people ("Elena, Jan, good to see you both") so neither has to admit forgetting; conversational slips are given quiet time for self-repair.

Violated: remarks such as "You've met before, don't you remember?" or "You told me that already"; finishing a resident's sentence prematurely; pairing residents merely to occupy them.
)))
|Source|Kitwood, T. (1997). //Dementia Reconsidered: The Person Comes First//. Open University Press. Goffman, E. (1967). //Interaction Ritual//. Anchor Books.

= Human Value - Privacy [HV-03] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HV-03
|Name|Privacy
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.
|Situated meaning|(((
Each resident retains ownership over their life history, personal confidences, and quiet time in their bedroom. No personal detail about one resident is disclosed to another without explicit permission, presence in private bedrooms is tracked only at coarse resolution with separate consent, and all raw conversation processing remains inside the care home.

For care staff, privacy also means their daily work is not timed or monitored as workplace surveillance.
)))
|Held by|ST-01, ST-02, ST-05, ST-07 (high); ST-04, ST-06 (medium); ST-03 (low - relatives may wish to share more background than the resident themselves would release)
|Intrinsic or instrumental|Intrinsic; also supports Dignity (HV-02) and Autonomy (HV-01).
|Recognition criteria|(((
Served: only consented profile items are used for matching or icebreakers; no raw audio or transcripts are stored after a session; bedroom time is logged only at coarse daily resolution for residents who opted in.

Violated: disclosing a private fact or family matter to a peer; explaining a match by citing an unreleased detail; continuous or unconsented room monitoring.
)))
|Source|Friedman, B., Kahn, P. H., Jr. & Borning, A. (2008). Value Sensitive Design and Information Systems. In K. E. Himma & H. T. Tavani (Eds.), //The Handbook of Information and Computer Ethics//. Wiley. Niemeijer et al. (2010).

= Human Value - Safety [HV-04] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HV-04
|Name|Safety
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.
|Situated meaning|Residents remain physically and emotionally safe during robot sessions and peer meetings. Suggested activities respect each resident's mobility and clinical boundaries, and any sign of acute agitation, distress, or physical hazard triggers an immediate pause and human handover.
|Held by|ST-01, ST-04, ST-06, ST-07 (high)
|Intrinsic or instrumental|Instrumental: a prerequisite for social activities in the home.
|Recognition criteria|(((
Served: shared activities match the residents' assessed mobility and clinical guidance; robot and peer sessions pause immediately upon distress or agitation, with care staff notified promptly.

Violated: suggesting an activity that exceeds a resident's mobility limits; continuing a session when a resident shows agitation or distress.
)))
|Source|Cohen-Mansfield, J., Marx, M. S. & Rosenthal, A. S. (1989). A description of agitation in a nursing home. //Journal of Gerontology//, 44(3), M77-M84. Härlein, J. et al. (2009). Fall risk factors in older people with dementia or cognitive impairment. //Journal of Advanced Nursing//, 65(5), 922-933.

= Human Value - Well-being [HV-05] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HV-05
|Name|Well-being
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.
|Situated meaning|Emotional comfort, calm, and days that hold pleasant company and something to look forward to, free from social pressure, embarrassment, agitation, or blunt reminders of painful losses.
|Held by|ST-01 to ST-06 (high); ST-07 (medium)
|Intrinsic or instrumental|Intrinsic.
|Recognition criteria|(((
Served: observable pleasure and alertness during and after conversations; reduced agitation and distress over time.

Violated: anxiety, frustration, or agitation triggered by an awkward introduction, a public refusal, or an insensitive remark.
)))
|Source|Lawton, M. P., Van Haitsma, K. & Klapper, J. (1996). Observed affect in nursing home residents with Alzheimer's disease. //Journal of Gerontology B//, 51(1), P3-P14. Woods et al. (2018).

= Human Value - Social connectedness [HV-06] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HV-06
|Name|Social connectedness
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.
|Situated meaning|Residents enjoy meaningful conversation and companionship with compatible peers in the care home and, over time, build mutual friendships that ease loneliness and give them a reason to spend time in communal areas.
|Held by|ST-01, ST-02, ST-03, ST-05 (high); ST-04 (medium)
|Intrinsic or instrumental|Both: valued in its own right, and instrumental in motivating residents to leave their bedrooms and engage with daily life.
|Recognition criteria|(((
Served: introductions lead to repeat conversations and shared time in common areas; residents can name a peer in the home whom they enjoy talking with.

Violated: using the robot as a substitute for human company; one-off introductions that never lead to further contact; social withdrawal following a pressured or awkward meeting.
)))
|Source|Victor, C. R. (2012). Loneliness in care homes: a neglected area of research? //Aging Health//, 8(6), 637-646. Holwerda et al. (2014). de Medeiros et al. (2012).

= Human Value - Identity [HV-07] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HV-07
|Name|Identity
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.
|Situated meaning|Residents are recognised as the people they have been throughout their lives—defined by their hometowns, trades, eras, musical tastes, and personal stories rather than by their diagnosis. Shared life history gives two residents natural common ground for conversation.
|Held by|ST-01, ST-03, ST-05 (high)
|Intrinsic or instrumental|Intrinsic; also instrumental in supplying effortless topics for peer conversation.
|Recognition criteria|(((
Served: introductions and icebreakers draw directly on the residents' own life stories, interests, and preferred eras.

Violated: getting biographical facts wrong; matching residents solely by clinical stage or dining table rather than shared background and interests.
)))
|Source|Kitwood (1997). Woods, B. et al. (2018). Reminiscence therapy for dementia. //Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews//, (3), CD001120.

= Human Value - Truthfulness [HV-08] =

**Report Format:** §1.2 Human Values and Human Factors

== Fields ==

|=Field|=Description
|ID|HV-08
|Name|Truthfulness
|Status|In Progress
|Evidence basis|Provisional; based on published literature and group reasoning.
|Situated meaning|(((
No deception by design: the System never tells lies (including well-meant "therapeutic" lies), never pretends to be human or to share human memories, and never plays along with a false belief. Once trust is broken in dementia care, it is rarely recovered.

Because profile entries can become outdated and speech recognition can mishear an utterance, the System states biographical facts only from verified profile items, speaks conservatively when uncertain, and corrects itself plainly when contradicted.

Painful truths—such as why the resident lives in the home, a bereavement, or a diagnosis—are always handed over to a trusted human caregiver rather than answered by the robot.
)))
|Held by|ST-01, ST-03, ST-04, ST-06 (high)
|Intrinsic or instrumental|Intrinsic; also instrumental because trust is a precondition for accepting social facilitation.
|Recognition criteria|(((
Served: dialogue contains no untruths, denials, or false pretence; factual errors are acknowledged immediately and flagged for correction; questions on sensitive topics are deferred to the designated trusted person.

Violated: telling a resident "You are only staying here for a few days"; leaving a factual error uncorrected; the robot delivering news of a bereavement or diagnosis.
)))
|Source|Mental Health Foundation (2016). //What Is Truth?// James et al. (2006). Wang et al. (2026).

